\section{Elliptic curves and the sums of the three cubes problem}

In this section, we generate infinitely many rational solutions for an open case of the sums of the three cubes problem, $k = 114$.

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{$k = 114$}{k = 114}}

\subsubsection{Modular analysis}

We then make a modular analysis (with the computational assistance of SageMath) of the fraction $\dfrac{36n^3 - 19}{\alpha}$ to determine values of $(n, \alpha)$ for which the aforementioned fraction yield integer solutions. It was then determined that $n \equiv a\pmod{p}$ where $a,p$ are positive integers. Within the range of $p < 20$ for instance, $n \equiv 4{\pmod5}, n \equiv {10\pmod{11}}, n \equiv {1\pmod{17}}$ and $n \equiv 0\mod19$ \cite{Agbanwa_s3c_particularly_for_114}.
Further searches found a promising value for $n$, $n \equiv 2094194294170672539119 \pmod{3782234599225650235415}$ which can be expressed alternatively as $n = 3782234599225650235415 \cdot k_1 +  2094194294170672539119$ \cite{agbanwa_congruence_screening}. \\

Recall that
\begin{equation*}
    m = \dfrac{r^3 - k}{6}
\end{equation*}
Considering the specific case of $k=114$,
\begin{equation*}
    m = \dfrac{r^3 - 114}{6}
\end{equation*}
solutions to the above equation are of the form, $m = 36n^3 - 19$ and $r = 6n$.

Equation \eqref{2.113} can be rewritten as:

\begin{equation}\label{5.98}
     P^{2} = -Q^{3} + 36n^2 \cdot Q^2 - 2 (6n)(36n^3 - 19)  \cdot Q + (36n^3 - 19)^2
\end{equation}

For arbitrary values of $n \geq 1$, we calculate their corresponding rational points $(Q,P)$.\\

\subsubsection{\texorpdfstring{Solving equation \eqref{2.113} for $n \equiv{1\pmod{17}}$}{Solving equation 2.17 for n equivalent (1 mod 17)}}

When $n = 1$, the resulting equation is:

\begin{equation}\label{5.991}
    P^2 = -Q^3 + 36\cdot Q^2 - 204 \cdot Q + 289
\end{equation}

whose rational point is $(Q,P) = (-6, -55)$ \cite{agbanwa_elliptic_arbitrary_2024}.


To ascertain the values for $x,y,z$, recall from page 7 that:

\begin{align*}
    x &= \dfrac{m + P}{Q}\\
      &= \dfrac{(36(1)^3 - 19) - 55}{-6}\\
      &= \dfrac{19}{3}\\
    y &= \dfrac{m - P}{Q}\\
      &= \dfrac{(36(1)^3 - 19) + 55}{-6}\\
      &= -12\\
    z &= r - \dfrac{2 \cdot m}{Q}\\
      &= 6(1)- \dfrac{2 \cdot (36(1)^3 - 19)}{-6}\\
      &= \dfrac{35}{3}
\end{align*}

\begin{equation*}
    (\dfrac{19}{3})^3 + (-12)^3 + (\dfrac{35}{3})^3 = 114
\end{equation*}
Equation \eqref{5.991} is subsequently transformed to this form:

\begin{equation}\label{5.997}
    P^2 = X^3 + 36\cdot X^2 + 204 \cdot X + 289
\end{equation}
 with reference to equation \eqref{2.115} where $Q \rightarrow -X$.

 Other multiples of the above rational solution can be found computationally using Magma \cite{agbanwa_modulo_17}. The code can be tweaked to generate as many multiples as needed.\\

 
When $n = 18$, the resulting equation is:

\begin{equation}\label{5.992}
    P^2 = -Q^3 + 11664 \cdot Q^2 - 45345528 \cdot Q + 44071864489
\end{equation}

whose rational point is $(Q,P) = \left(-\dfrac{185089608}{361}, -\dfrac{2546797361041}{6859}\right)$ \cite{agbanwa_elliptic_arbitrary_2024}.\\

To ascertain the values for $x,y,z$,

\begin{align*}
    x &= \dfrac{m + P}{Q}\\
      &= \dfrac{(36(18)^3 - 19) +\dfrac{2546797361041}{6859}}{-\dfrac{185089608}{361}}\\
      &= -\dfrac{8935164}{12331}\\
    y &= \dfrac{m - P}{Q}\\
      &= \dfrac{(36(18)^3 - 19) - \dfrac{2546797361041}{6859}}{-\dfrac{185089608}{361}}\\
      &= \dfrac{495073}{684}\\
    z &= r - \dfrac{2 \cdot m}{Q}\\
      &= 6(18)- \dfrac{2 \cdot (36(18)^3 - 19)}{-\dfrac{185089608}{361}}\\
      &= \dfrac{2542445}{23364}
\end{align*}

\begin{equation*}
    \left(-\dfrac{8935164}{12331}\right)^3 + \left(\dfrac{495073}{684}\right)^3 + \left(\dfrac{2542445}{23364}\right)^3 = 114
\end{equation*}

Similar to the previous case, equation \eqref{5.992} is transformed to:

\begin{equation}\label{5.9931}
    P^2 = X^3 + 11664 \cdot X^2 + 45345528 \cdot X + 44071864489
\end{equation}

Other multiples of the above rational solution can be found computationally using Magma \cite{agbanwa_modulo_17}. The code can be tweaked to generate as many multiples as needed.\\


When $n = 35$, the resulting equation is:

\begin{equation}\label{5.993}
    P^2 = -Q^3 + 44100 \cdot Q^2 - 648262020 \cdot Q + 2382333597361
\end{equation}

whose rational point is $(Q,P) = \left(\dfrac{18579428171370729554967180}{5940560614682397752161}, \dfrac{398012845566067230292856202887488879471}{457868901666652343402341914699409}\right)$ \cite{agbanwa_elliptic_arbitrary_2024}.

We substitute the above values into the formulas for $x,y,z$,

\begin{align*}
    x &= \dfrac{m + P}{Q}\\
      &= \dfrac{(36(35)^3 - 19) + \dfrac{398012845566067230292856202887488879471}{457868901666652343402341914699409}}{\dfrac{18579428171370729554967180}{5940560614682397752161}}\\
      &= \dfrac{317327857679685063263370}{411339254811547296857}\\
    y &= \dfrac{m - P}{Q}\\
      &= \dfrac{(36(35)^3 - 19) - \dfrac{398012845566067230292856202887488879471}{457868901666652343402341914699409}}{\dfrac{18579428171370729554967180}{5940560614682397752161}}\\
      &= \dfrac{18737758844444511451853}{86921724611539618470}\\
    z &= r - \dfrac{2 \cdot m}{\dfrac{18579428171370729554967180}{5940560614682397752161}}\\
      &= 6(35)- \dfrac{2 \cdot (36(35)^3 - 19)}{\dfrac{18579428171370729554967180}{5940560614682397752161}}\\
      &= -\dfrac{4676638377872274010261}{6018677318143446390}
\end{align*}
\begin{equation*}
    \left(\dfrac{317327857679685063263370}{411339254811547296857}\right)^3 + \left( \dfrac{18737758844444511451853}{86921724611539618470}\right)^3 + \left(-\dfrac{4676638377872274010261}{6018677318143446390}\right)^3 = 114
\end{equation*}

The above sum can also be verified with this Magma code \cite{agbanwa_magma_verification_2026}.

We transform equation \eqref{5.993} to:

\begin{equation}\label{5.9932}
    P^2 = X^3 + 44100 \cdot X^2 + 648262020 \cdot X + 2382333597361
\end{equation}

where $Q \rightarrow -X$. 

More multiples of the rational solution to equation \eqref{5.9932} were generated computationally on Magma, \cite{agbanwa_modulo_17}.

When $n = 52$, the resulting equation is:

\begin{equation}\label{5.99991}
     P^2 = -Q^3 + 97344 \cdot Q^2 - 3158606256 \cdot Q + 25622517773161
\end{equation}

The growing complexity of the resulting elliptic curve equation makes the computational search for the rank and generators infeasible for personal computers.


\subsubsection{\texorpdfstring{Solving equation \eqref{3.03} for a fixed $\alpha$}{Solving equation 2.39 for a fixed alpha}}

In this subsection, we substitute a specific case of $\alpha$ into equation \eqref{5.909}, in search for rational solutions to \eqref{5.909} and by extension, rational solutions to $k = 114$.

Equation \eqref{3.03} can be re-expressed as,

\begin{equation} \label{5.99}
    \Phi = -(\alpha + 6n) + \sqrt{(\alpha + 6n)^2 +  \dfrac{36n^3 - 19}{\alpha}}
\end{equation}
Let
\begin{equation}\label{6.0}
  Y^2  = (\alpha + 6n)^2 +  \dfrac{36n^3 - 19}{\alpha}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation} \label{5.909}
    Y^2  = \dfrac{36}{\alpha} \cdot n^3 + 36n^2 + 12 \alpha \cdot n + \alpha^2  - \dfrac{19}{\alpha}
\end{equation}
.

Suppose $\alpha = 19$, equation \eqref{6.0} is re-expressible as:
\begin{align*}
    Y^2  &= (19 + 6n)^2 + \dfrac{36}{19} \cdot n^3 - 1 \\
    Y^2  &= 361 + 228\cdot n + 36n^2 + \dfrac{36}{19} \cdot n^3 - 1
\end{align*}
\begin{equation}\label{6.15}
    Y^2 = \dfrac{36}{19} \cdot n^3 + 36n^2 + 228\cdot n + 360
\end{equation}
On using the computational assistance of Magma \cite{agbanwa_sums_cubes_2025}, a rational point satisfying equation \eqref{6.15} was found, 

\begin{multline}\label{6.16}
(n, Y) = (-\dfrac{864648976084347353011927145714245480219355}{635859049050585085274882901637910426578284}, \\
-\dfrac{3894228350436067038312065162575538689415800674650195219149913735}{368355224867664550854516818920804459714526459420694554473909596})
\end{multline}
 
\begin{align*}
    x &= - 19 -\dfrac{3894228350436067038312065162575538689415800674650195219149913735}{368355224867664550854516818920804459714526459420694554473909596}\\
    &= -\dfrac{10892977622921693504547884722070823423991803403643391754154196059}{368355224867664550854516818920804459714526459420694554473909596}\\
  y  &= - 19 + \dfrac{3894228350436067038312065162575538689415800674650195219149913735}{368355224867664550854516818920804459714526459420694554473909596}\\
    &= -\dfrac{3104520922049559427923754396919746045160202054343001315854368589}{368355224867664550854516818920804459714526459420694554473909596}\\
 z &= 2 \cdot (19) + 6 \cdot \left(-\dfrac{864648976084347353011927145714245480219355}{635859049050585085274882901637910426578284}\right)\\
   &= \dfrac{3162458334569358187062331231325853888109777}{105976508175097514212480483606318404429714}
\end{align*}

\begin{multline*}
     \left(-\dfrac{10892977622921693504547884722070823423991803403643391754154196059}{368355224867664550854516818920804459714526459420694554473909596}\right)^3 +  \\
     \left(-\dfrac{3104520922049559427923754396919746045160202054343001315854368589}{368355224867664550854516818920804459714526459420694554473909596}\right)^3 +  \\
     \left(\dfrac{3162458334569358187062331231325853888109777}{105976508175097514212480483606318404429714}\right)^3 = 114
\end{multline*}

The above results have been computationally verified to satisfy 114 \cite{agbanwa_magma_code_2025}. Given that only one rational point was yielded, point doubling can be used to generate more points, $2P,3P,4P,5P,...$ and by extension more rational solutions to $k = 114$. With the computational assistance of SageMath, several multiples were found \cite{agbanwa_sagemath_point_generation_2026}. 

On substituting $n = 17$ into equation \eqref{6.0}, the resulting equation is:

\begin{align*}
    Y ^2  &= (17 + 6n)^2 + \dfrac{36}{17} \cdot n^3 - \dfrac{19}{17} \\
    Y ^2  &= 289 + 204 \cdot n + 36n^2 + \dfrac{36}{19} \cdot n^3 - \dfrac{19}{17}\\
\end{align*}
\begin{equation}\label{6.151}
    Y^2 = \dfrac{36}{17} \cdot n^3 + 36n^2 + 204 \cdot n + \dfrac{4894}{17}
\end{equation}
Computationally searching for rational points on Magma returned no solutions \cite{agbanwa_magma_alpha17_202466}. The rank of equation \eqref{6.151} was computationally determined to be $0$ \cite{Agbanwa_Magma_Elliptic_Rank_17}. 

Aside from $\alpha =  19$, which yielded the point $\left(n, Y\right)$ which generates rational points for equation \eqref{5.99}, we employed the computational assistance of SageMath to search for values of $\alpha$ for which equation \eqref{5.909} has rank $0$ or $ \geq 1$ for the first $50$ values of $\alpha$ \cite{agbanwa_sagemath_rank_2026}. The values of $\alpha$ which have a rank $\geq 1$ observably follow no predictable pattern and hence, the difficulty in generating a recursive pattern for $(n, Y)$ for arbitrary values of $\alpha$.


